From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000007 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iggy <iggy@triform.ai>
Date: Sun, 17 May 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cloud-browser: sink-attachment-driven capture lifecycle
 (M2-R5 / CV2-40)

Wires the lifecycle of `viz::FrameSinkVideoCapturer` (the producer half
that R2 created `mojo::Remote`s for and R3 owns inside its track source)
to the WebRTC sink-attachment lifecycle of
`CbFrameSinkVideoTrackSource`. Result: the viz capturer only runs while
at least one webrtc `VideoSinkInterface` is attached, and frames stop
flowing as soon as the last sink detaches.

Why this patch exists:
- M2-R3 ("CV2-38 — CloudBrowserFrameSinkVideoTrackSource adapter")
  introduces a track source that wraps a viz `FrameSinkVideoCapturer`
  and re-emits captured frames as `webrtc::VideoFrame`s through the
  inherited `rtc::AdaptedVideoTrackSource::OnFrame()` channel. R3 left
  open the question "when does the producer actually start?" — the
  obvious answer ("at construction") wastes CPU/GPU whenever the
  cloud-browser tab is constructed but no peer has subscribed yet.
- The standard libwebrtc shape for a lazily-started source is the
  pattern used by `webrtc::VideoTrackSource` and many embedder sources
  (e.g. Chromium's `MediaStreamVideoSource` in blink/): override
  `AddOrUpdateSink` / `RemoveSink`, count sinks, and Start/Stop the
  underlying producer on the 0->1 and 1->0 transitions. This makes
  capture implicitly peer-driven — once M3-R5's track-add fires in the
  WebRTC offer/answer, the peer's `RtpReceiver` attaches its decoder
  sink, sink_count_ flips from 0 to 1, and the viz producer starts;
  on `PeerConnection::Close` the sink detaches and the producer stops.
- The capturer mojo `Remote` is sequence-affine (it was bound on a
  specific `SequencedTaskRunner` by R3), but
  `AddOrUpdateSink`/`RemoveSink` are called from arbitrary WebRTC
  threads (worker and signaling). All sink-count mutations and
  producer calls are therefore bounced onto R3's capturer task runner,
  guarded by a `SEQUENCE_CHECKER`, and made idempotent so that
  spurious double-Start/double-Stop after a transient 0->1->0 race
  cannot deadlock or leak viz pool buffers.

The buffer-pool drain on Stop is required by the
`viz::mojom::FrameSinkVideoConsumer` contract: every
`OnFrameCaptured(..., callbacks)` hands the consumer a pending Remote
to `FrameSinkVideoConsumerFrameCallbacks` whose `Done()` method MUST
fire before the underlying shared-image is recycled. Dropping the
`mojo::Receiver` on Stop without firing `Done()` for in-flight buffers
leaks viz pool slots until the renderer is torn down. R5 walks the
`in_flight_done_callbacks_` map that R3 already maintains keyed on
buffer_id and Run()s each callback, then clears the map.

R5 deliberately does NOT touch FrameSinkId resolution (still M2-R4)
or any CDP-method removal (deferred to the M6 ledger). The diagnostic
`Cb.startFrameSinkCapture` CDP path that R4 keeps remains live; once
a peer has attached a sink, R5's first-sink Start() races with R4's
explicit Start() and the idempotency guard (`capturer_running_`)
serializes them.

If M3 Refinement determines that the capturer must pre-warm BEFORE any
peer offer (e.g. so the first frame after offer/answer is recent
rather than stale), the policy flips: R5's lazy Start becomes a
boot-driven Start and the sink-attach hook becomes a no-op. Re-derive
when M3 lands; the FLAG in CV2-40 covers this.

Touched files (NOTE — applies on top of M2-R3 patch / branch
cv2/m2-track-source-adapter; the cb_framesink_video_track_source.{h,cc}
and its unittest target are introduced by R3):

